{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's explicit instruction, 2026-10-09: write a dated note with an implementable design for Artroom's live developer interface, targeted for builder to implement. Prioritize simplicity, especially in the TypeScript API, and convergence across the interface protocols.\n\nAGREED DIRECTION\nMake the primary developer experience “observe a scope, invoke acts, stay current.” Support recorded applications and zero-durability volatile applications through the same understandable interaction model. Request/response remains useful for individual act results; live observation supplies ongoing authorized state. WebSockets are the preferred initial transport for frequent bidirectional interaction, but the public application model must not depend on WebSocket framing. HTTP with streaming can provide live interaction too; plain HTTP and agent/MCP clients need honest snapshot/wait capabilities.\n\nDELIVERABLE AND OWNERSHIP\nPublish a dated repository note, suggested path notes/2026-10-09-live-developer-interface.md (use the actual delivery date if later), with concrete decisions and a source-level implementation plan that builder can follow without another architecture round. Design delivery is the task here; source implementation is the subsequent builder handoff. Reconcile existing ownership rather than commission competing session/watch implementations. Name the builder slices, dependencies, acceptance and appropriate scheduling. Keep current correctness repairs and coordinated review/gate priorities intact.\n\nRead the current successor source and current owned proposals, not just the earlier baseline. Reconcile notes/2026-10-09-shared-language-volatile-acts.md, especially sections 5, 6, 7 and 9; durability infrastructure request 578edf653772749b5fa9b47f6782f6db4d0ab720; counting demo request 4073e7561b47b3b13aaceb616d9fc3d67478af5e and its current handoffs; existing head-aware-query, responsiveness and authority/session work. Record exact inspected source heads and artifact identities and distinguish current implementation from proposed support. The live interface is shared infrastructure, not a second volatile-act language or a replacement recorded executor.\n\n1. SMALL TYPESCRIPT API\nChoose and write actual proposed public types, signatures and short realistic examples. Prefer one clear scope/application handle, a small discover/read/invoke/watch vocabulary, one chosen watch consumption style, and explicit cleanup. Reuse existing handles, typed refs, signers, result types and submit/settle machinery wherever possible. The exact names and callback versus async-iterator choice are planner decisions: select one supported approach, not a menu of equivalent wrappers.\n\nShow the ordinary path with minimal setup: authenticate/open or obtain a handle, observe authorized state, invoke a permitted act, render its result, and close. Developers should not need to build socket routing, interpret raw frames, manually coordinate snapshot/subscribe races, or learn a new session/client class for every transport or execution profile. Keep advanced transport selection and diagnostics optional. Avoid a generic event bus, transport factory hierarchy, reactive framework dependency, or a proliferation of managers and configuration switches unless a concrete requirement proves necessary.\n\nUse accurate typed distinctions for recorded and volatile results without multiplying the top-level API. Explain who owns expected revisions and how stale forms behave; automatic refresh must not silently retarget or resubmit the user's act. Define where unresolved recorded requests retain their exact signed request and how callers reconcile them. Do not equate a current view with a settled request.\n\n2. ONE SEMANTIC CONTRACT, THIN ADAPTERS\nSpecify the shared operations, identities, response/error forms and watch observations once. Map them concretely to WebSocket, HTTP streaming, plain HTTP/bounded waits, TypeScript service-binding/API use, and MCP tools/resources/notifications as appropriate. Inventory what actually exists versus what is owed. Keep authorization, validation, admission, revision checks and result construction at their common owning boundary; adapters should translate framing and lifecycle, not reimplement application rules.\n\nGive a compact capability matrix: invoke, snapshot, watch/invalidation, bounded wait, continuity/resume aid and unsolicited notification. Identify the smallest first supported set and explicitly deferred adapters. Do not mandate equivalent latency or complete historical delivery across protocols. HTTP is not inherently non-push, and MCP is not inherently request/response-only: verify the actual negotiated MCP version and deployed host behavior. A protocol notification is not a promise that an agent will be scheduled to act. The MCP interface must remain useful through explicit reads and bounded waits when host notification support is absent.\n\nSpecify request correlation, framing/version negotiation, ordering scope and cancellation/close meanings. Reuse established canonical envelopes and bounds. If one connection multiplexes scopes/regions/watches, state how each retains its own authorization and identity; a connection-level sequence must not invent cross-scope order. Keep the initial wire protocol small.\n\n3. WATCH AND LOCAL VIEW LIFECYCLE\nChoose whether each first slice uses authoritative snapshots plus invalidations or typed deltas; do not require a full incremental patch engine where head invalidation and affected-view refresh suffice. Recorded head streams plus authorized resnapshot should be a useful first slice independently of the volatile executor.\n\nDefine the initial authorized snapshot and atomic snapshot-to-watch handoff. Every later mutation is either covered by the snapshot or offered after its watermark while continuity holds. Account for asynchronous authorization/enrichment, pagination/completeness, a head changing during a read, changes arriving during refresh, and concurrent watches without an unbounded full-history reconstruction.\n\nKeep communication availability, observation freshness/completeness, and individual act outcome separate. Specify minimal public lifecycle states and transitions for connecting, current, disconnected/stale, reset/gap, expired/revoked and closed as needed; do not create a large state taxonomy without user-visible value. Define credential refresh, bounded reconnect/backoff, cancellation, disposal and resource limits. A live view should automatically refresh affected data; no manual refresh loop or blanket redraw should be the normal application code.\n\nSlow readers and overflow must expose a gap/reset or end the watch. Presence views may coalesce; retained message bodies cannot silently coalesce into a claim of complete delivery. Resuming a bounded (generation, revision) aid is optional, and missing history demands resnapshot. Polling clients may miss transient acts; absence from a snapshot proves neither non-occurrence nor successful delivery.\n\n4. DURABILITY AND REQUEST RECOVERY\nTransport choice must not select durability. Recorded acts preserve existing signatures, grants, expected revisions, journal commitment/output gate, receipts and exact-request submit/settle recovery. A recorded head observed over a socket is not the receipt for a particular command.\n\nVolatile acts remain ZERO durability: Applied means local application at generation G/revision N; all state, messages, timers and dedup aids may disappear immediately. Keep live identities separate from recorded facts/heads. Lost volatile response followed by reset remains unknown. No implicit durable inbox, offline act queue, transparent replay of speech after reset, exactly-once promise, or conversion of a live request into a new generation. A new-generation act requires the declared rejoin/new-invocation policy. State retry ownership and limits clearly.\n\nConnection survival is separate from generation survival. Verify Cloudflare hibernation behavior: sockets can remain open while in-memory application state is lost. Specify what the server sends and what clients discard/rejoin when that happens, with a feasible first implementation and explicit operational tradeoffs.\n\n5. AUTHORITY, SESSIONS AND PRESENCE\nReuse current authentication and distinguish the existing read-only session credential from proposed live action authority and public session ownership. Recorded acts remain signed by their legitimate actor. A socket ID or read token must not silently become write authority.\n\nDescribe trusted invocation context, full scope/incarnation/application/region binding, action/read revalidation and expiry/revocation across every adapter. Keep credentials out of URLs, public handles, snapshots and logs. Multiple tabs/devices/agents have explicit session ownership. HTTP requests can participate in a bounded application session without reentering presence on every call.\n\nPresence renew is an application act under its declared policy, not an automatic consequence of a transport ping. Leases handle missed disconnects; transport reopen must not silently restore user attention or ownership. Reuse the declared presence/message semantics rather than add mandatory separate chat or signal APIs. Existing content addresses remain subjects; do not invent arbitrary topics to simplify routing.\n\n6. BUILDER HANDOFF AND ACCEPTANCE\nGive exact files/packages and existing symbols to reuse or change; proposed public contract/wire types; responsibilities and state-machine pseudocode for the tricky handoffs; compatibility/migration treatment of existing HTTP/client/CLI/Page callers; dependency order and ownership. Choose reasonable initial bounds from current constraints and state their enforcement, rather than invent a new tuning project.\n\nProvide a short decisive acceptance matrix at meaningful boundaries:\n- recorded snapshot/watch sees a concurrent change without a missed handoff;\n- view refresh cannot settle an unresolved act or alter its confirmation barrier;\n- recorded lost-response reconciliation keeps the same signed request;\n- volatile generation loss is visible even on a still-open socket;\n- slow-reader gaps and incomplete reads never claim current/complete delivery;\n- expiry/revocation and session ownership hold across adapters;\n- bounded-wait/poll clients remain useful without implied transient history;\n- cancellation/close/reconnect release resources and do not duplicate application effects;\n- two clients automatically converge on authorized recorded state, while volatile reset discards obsolete state.\n\nUse a recorded interaction (counting or a development/review flow) and presence/message interaction to demonstrate the same small TypeScript affordances. Include one polling/MCP example. Identify meaningful focused tests per docs/testing.md and one coordinated source gate before review when builder implements; no mutation sweeps, repeated whole suites or checkout package installs for probes. Any latency measurement should be a bounded same-deployment comparison, not a benchmark project.\n\nCOMPLETION\nDeliver one coherent dated design note and an actionable builder handoff with selected API/protocol choices, source map, implementation slices, acceptance, compatibility and explicit unsupported capabilities. Explain the major simplifications and tradeoffs in plain English. Open owner decisions must have an owner and resolution point; do not leave the essential TypeScript shape or watch/recovery model as alternatives for builder to design. Report the exact reviewed note/artifact and coordinate the implementation commission through the existing planner/builder workflow.","body":{"conditions":"Publish a dated, concrete live developer interface design note for builder implementation. Select a small TypeScript API and shared semantic contract across WebSocket, HTTP streaming, polling/bounded waits, service bindings and MCP; specify snapshot/watch, authority, lifecycle, recorded versus volatile results/recovery, compatibility, source-level slices and decisive acceptance. Reconcile existing durability/counting/session ownership and current priorities; design delivery here, subsequent builder source commission.","target_head":"25467a36901817f18c792d350dede9765546168e","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}